@title What Eighteen Months of Four-Day Weeks Did to Our Support Team
@author Priscilla Adeyemi-Koh, Head of Customer Support
@date 2026-10-18

In October we made a decision I would not have predicted three years ago: the four-day week is now permanent for the Halden Cartography support team. The pilot ran from 2025-04-07 to 2026-10-02, eighteen months in all. I want to write down what we learned while it is still fresh, including the parts that did not go smoothly, because when we were deciding whether to try it, I went looking for honest accounts and found mostly slogans.

## Why we tried it

Our support team is 23 people. In 2024, annual attrition was 21%. That means that in a typical year we were saying goodbye to four or five colleagues and spending months hiring and training their replacements. When I read the exit interviews side by side, one reason kept coming up more than any other: burnout.

The feeling people described was that the week never really ended. Support work is relentless in a quiet way. There is always another ticket, and the people who care the most are the ones who struggle hardest to stop.

So I asked a different question. Instead of making the work smaller, what if we made the week shorter, and protected the time off seriously enough that it actually felt like time off?

## How the schedule works

The rules are simple, and I think that is part of why they held up.

Everyone works Monday to Thursday, 9 hours a day. That is 36 hours a week, at full pay. Nobody took a salary cut, and we were clear about that from the first conversation.

Friday still needs coverage, because our customers do not stop needing help on Fridays. That comes from a rotating on-call group of 4 people. Whoever is on the Friday rotation takes the following Monday off instead, so they still get a three-day break, just shifted. The rotation means nobody carries Fridays for long, and everyone understands the coverage from both sides.

## What happened to the numbers

I expected customer metrics to slip a little and was prepared to argue that a happier team was worth it. That argument turned out to be unnecessary.

Our median first response time fell from 3.4 hours to 2.9 hours. Customer satisfaction rose from 4.3 to 4.5 out of 5. I still find those two numbers slightly surprising, and my best explanation is that rested people triage better. They pick up the right ticket first, they write clearer replies, and they make fewer of the small mistakes that turn one reply into three.

The team numbers moved more. Annual attrition fell from 21% to 9%. Sick days fell by 31%. Those two numbers are what convinced our leadership, and honestly they are what convinced me. Fewer departures means less time spent hiring, fewer half-trained colleagues, and a team that knows our product and our customers deeply.

I want to be careful here. Eighteen months is a real stretch of time, but it is one team in one company. Plenty of other things changed in that period too. I am confident the schedule was the biggest factor; I am not claiming it was the only one.

## What got harder

Not everything got easier, and I would be doing other managers a disservice if I left this part out.

**Thursday evening handoffs.** With the week compressed, Thursday afternoon became the moment everything had to be passed on cleanly to the Friday group. It is the most fragile point in the week, and it deserves more attention than we first gave it.

**Enterprise customers who wanted Friday phones.** Two of our enterprise customers wanted guaranteed Friday phone support, not just ticket coverage. The on-call group of 4 could not absorb that on top of everything else, so we hired 2 more people to cover it. That is a real cost, and it is worth knowing about before you start.

**Long days in winter.** A few people found 9-hour days tiring in the winter months, when it is dark on both ends of the workday. I do not have a neat answer to this one yet, and I would rather say so than pretend otherwise.

## If you try it

If you are thinking about a four-day week for your own team, here is what I would tell you over coffee.

1. **Pilot for at least 6 months.** The first few weeks feel wonderful and the next few feel chaotic. You only learn what the schedule is really like once the novelty has worn off and the hard weeks have come and gone.
2. **Write the coverage rules before the first week.** Who covers Friday, who gets which day off in exchange, and what happens when someone is sick. If you leave these questions to be settled on the fly, the most conscientious people end up quietly covering the gaps, and you recreate the burnout you were trying to fix.
3. **Measure customer metrics alongside team morale.** Morale alone will not convince anyone who is worried about customers, and customer metrics alone will miss the reason you started. Track both from the start so you can tell the whole story.

Eighteen months in, I am glad we took the risk. The team is calmer, our customers are better served, and I spend far less of my time saying goodbye.
